  "revcopy_note": "You can edit the text below before copying. For Gemini Notebook, mark few cards at a time so the prompt stays small.",
  "revcopy_copy": "Copy this text",
  "revcopy_done": "Copied! Paste it in the AI chat, ask for the fix, copy its answer and come back to the “Paste fix” button.",
  "rev_prompt_full": "Improve the EXISTING flashcards below, WITHOUT creating new cards or changing each one's topic. Complement and improve each card: fix errors, shorten long answers and turn statements into questions where it makes sense. KEEP the exact format — one card per line, blank line between cards, \"Question :: Answer :: tags\", cloze {{c1::...}}, title on an \"@\" line above the card, explanation on a \"+\" line below. Reply ONLY with the corrected cards, in the same format.\n\nMANDATORY FORMAT RULES (the app rejects anything different):\na) BLANK NUMBERING: each blank in the SAME card needs a DIFFERENT number — c1, c2, c3… NEVER repeat the same number in one card (two c1 blanks are hidden together and the card becomes unanswerable). If the question already has {{c1::...}}, the answer uses {{c2::...}}.\nb) TITLE: the \"@ Title\" line stands ALONE, ABOVE the card. Never on the same line as the question.\n   WRONG:  @ Topic X What is the rule? :: Answer\n   RIGHT:  @ Topic X\n           What is the rule? :: Answer\nc) TAGS: keep the original tags, separated by COMMAS, with no spaces inside each tag (use _). E.g.: \"NBASP_100, ISSAI, Audit\".\nd) EXPLANATION (\"Learn more\"): EVERY explanation line starts with \"+\". No loose numbered lists, no lines with only \"*\" or \"+\", no blank lines inside the explanation.\ne) EXPLANATION READABILITY: one concept per \"+\" line, each short and complete. Start each item with the key term followed by \" — \". Use \"+ ---\" to separate blocks. Example:\n+ Qualified (except for) — material misstatements, but NOT pervasive.\n+ Adverse — misstatements are material AND pervasive.\n+ Disclaimer — insufficient evidence, both material and pervasive.\n+ ---\n+ Exam tip — the difference between qualified and adverse lies in pervasiveness.\n\nf) ONE LINE PER CARD: the WHOLE answer stays on the SAME line as the question. To list items use \" / \" or \"<br>\" — never split the answer across lines or use numbered lists inside it.\ng) NO MARKDOWN: no **bold**, ##, tables. To emphasize, use <b>text</b>.\n\nCARDS:\n{cards}\n\nGOLDEN RULE — EVERY CARD STANDS ALONE:\nWhoever answers this in two months won't have the source text. A card may never lean on it.\n- FORBIDDEN: \"Question 17\", \"Answer A\", \"the correct alternative\", \"answer key\", \"the item above\", \"statement I\", \"according to the text\", \"choose the option\", \"is wrong/right\" (without saying what).\n- The answer is the CONCEPT, never WHERE the concept was. Ask what a thing IS, what it DOES, when it APPLIES, how it DIFFERS from another.\n- Exam questions: throw away the stem, the numbering and the letters; keep the CONTENT. Each alternative becomes its own card with its own question.\n- When splitting a big card, REDISTRIBUTE — don't summarise. Every concept in the original must reappear as its own question; none may vanish.\n\n  WRONG: Which option correctly states the key trait of predictive methods? :: Answer D) full detailed planning\n  RIGHT: What characterises predictive methods? :: The whole scope is planned in detail before execution starts :: project_management\n  WRONG: What is the correct order of the definitions in Question 17? :: Answer A: (1) Evaluation, (2) Agenda setting, (3) Decision making\n  RIGHT: What is decided during agenda setting? :: Which problems, among many possible ones, make it onto the government's agenda :: public_policy\n",
  "rev_prompt_short": "Improve the flashcards below without creating new ones or changing each one's topic. Check them against this notebook's sources and fix content errors (articles, dates, values).\n\nFORMAT (mandatory):\n• The WHOLE answer on the SAME line as the question (items separated by \" / \" or \"<br>\"). No markdown (**bold**) — use <b>text</b>.\n• One card per line: \"Question :: Answer :: tags\", blank line between cards.\n• Blanks: {{c1::...}}. Each blank in the SAME card uses a DIFFERENT number (c1, c2, c3) — never repeat.\n• Title: an \"@ Title\" line alone, ABOVE the card (never on the same line).\n• Tags: the original ones, comma-separated, no inner spaces (use _).\n• Explanation: EVERY line starts with \"+\". One idea per line, starting with the term followed by \" — \". Use \"+ ---\" to separate blocks. No loose numbered lists or blank lines.\n\nReply only with the corrected cards.\n\nCARDS:\n{cards}\n\nGOLDEN RULE — EVERY CARD STANDS ALONE:\nWhoever answers this in two months won't have the source text. A card may never lean on it.\n- FORBIDDEN: \"Question 17\", \"Answer A\", \"the correct alternative\", \"answer key\", \"the item above\", \"statement I\", \"according to the text\", \"choose the option\", \"is wrong/right\" (without saying what).\n- The answer is the CONCEPT, never WHERE the concept was. Ask what a thing IS, what it DOES, when it APPLIES, how it DIFFERS from another.\n- Exam questions: throw away the stem, the numbering and the letters; keep the CONTENT. Each alternative becomes its own card with its own question.\n- When splitting a big card, REDISTRIBUTE — don't summarise. Every concept in the original must reappear as its own question; none may vanish.\n\n  WRONG: What is the correct order of the definitions in Question 17? :: Answer A: (1) Evaluation, (2) Agenda setting, (3) Decision making\n  RIGHT: What is decided during agenda setting? :: Which problems, among many possible ones, make it onto the government's agenda :: public_policy\n",
